"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was 4.1, and the surgical pathology revealed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and positive surgical margins, which are factors that increase risk. Conversely, the absence of extraprostatic extension and invasion of the seminal vesicles are favorable findings. Lymph node metastasis was present, which is a negative finding.\n\nThe difference between the biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted, with the biopsy showing an Acinar pattern, while the prostatectomy showed Gleason 3+4. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding the trajectory of recurrence. The nodal status was documented as positive metastasis. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and subsequent trajectory would be crucial to refine this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 64.18 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 51.35–77.02 months; its coverage has not been validated."
